## 3. Judgment calls (the instructor's — adopted, do not re-litigate)

**1. `charm` (bubbletea/lipgloss/bubbles) → Textual.** No Python binding to
Bubble Tea exists. Textual is the closest conceptual match to bubbletea's
reactive model/update/view loop, is pure Python with no native per-platform
build step, and ships a headless test harness (`App.run_test()` / `Pilot`) that
the Ruby setup has no equivalent of.

**2. `Thread#raise(Interrupt)` → cooperative cancellation.** Ruby's Esc handler
injects an exception into the turn thread wherever it is, including
mid-blocking-I/O. Python has no safe equivalent:
`PyThreadState_SetAsyncExc` only fires when the thread next returns to Python
bytecode, so it cannot cut short an HTTP call already in flight — it would take
effect once the call returns anyway, which is too late to matter. Rather than
ship a ctypes hack that *looks* like Ruby's behaviour and silently fails in the
one case that matters, add an optional `cancel_event` (`threading.Event`) to
`Agent`, checked at the top of each loop iteration, raising `TurnCancelled`.

> **Accepted gap, to be stated in the README and in review:** Esc will not
> interrupt a single in-flight backend call, only take effect at the next
> iteration or tool-call boundary. A deliberate documented divergence, not a
> missed port.

**3. Where the TUI launches from.** Ruby reaches the interactive TUI only
through the installed gem's `bin/boukensha` via `boukensha_loader.rb`, which
this port has excluded since step 10 (no Python gem/loader concept). Every
Python step's launcher runs `examples/example.py` directly, and since step 08
that file calls `repl()` interactively. **Keep that convention:** fold
`--no-tui` argv handling into `examples/example.py`, rather than inventing a
Python `boukensha_loader` to host a CLI flag. A one-line divergence in *where*
the flag is parsed, not in what it does.

